Penguicon, a combination science fiction convention and open source software expo in the greater Detroit metro area of Michigan, has allocated function space for Logfest 2006. In its fourth year, Penguicon is held April 21 through 23, 2006 at the Holiday Inn in Livonia, Michigan. See http://www.penguicon.org/ and http://wiki.penguicon.org/ for more information about Penguicon.

My presentation about Lojban at Penguicon 2005 packed the room, and is in demand for a repeat this year. There are at least four local Detroit-area jbopre who will be in attendance at Logfest 2006 activities, and probably more like seven. Please join us, and it will be very well attended!

A past Lojbanist, Eric S. Raymond, never fails to attend Penguicon. Guests of Honor this year will include:

Looney Labs, creators of Zendo and Nanofictionary, games which have been played at past Logfests

Chris DiBona, open source director for Google

Frank Hayes, filk musician and tech columnist for ComputerWorld

Steve Miller and Sharon Lee, authors of the Liaden universe novels

Program participants will include: Rob "CmdrTaco" Malda of Slashdot,  Tom Smith, The Great Luke Ski, John Scalzi, Karl Schroeder, Clif Flynt, "TheFerrett", Howard Tayler, Rob Balder

The preregistration period for Penguicon 4.0 will end at 11:59:59 pm EST March 31st, which is just over a week away. So, if you want to preregister and avoid the long, slower lines of the at the door registration (and save $5 per membership too), you should head over to our web site http://penguicon.org/ and preregister now.

Also, the hotel is filling up. There are plenty of overflow hotels next door, but reserve your room now to be close to the fun! The schedule is here: http://peanut.mtu.net/penguicon/ and even more events will be added.

Instead of getting one of the closed-off function space rooms, LogFest has a spacious and relaxing presentation area full of rows of chairs, which is open to the main public thoroughfare in the hotel. You can see it in the online photo tour: http://www.ichotelsgroup.com/h/d/hi/1/en/hd/dttln It's beneath the French-Quarter-style balconies, right next to the Holidome where the pool is, and across from the cafe.

I have requisitioned a projector with screen for LogFest, and a large whiteboard. We will attract tons of attention from the computer geeks and science fiction fans, and have that space for several hours each day to do with as we wish. We can write our own event schedule and I'll put it in the program book. 

-epkat